= Group cognition =

'''Group cognition''' is the study of cognitive processes that arise through interaction among members of a group. It examines how groups construct shared meanings, coordinate specialized knowledge, maintain common representations, divide cognitive work, make decisions, and solve problems over time.

The term does not imply that a group has one literal brain or consciousness. Group cognition is an analytical level: some cognitive processes can be understood only by examining interactions among participants rather than treating each individual independently.

Gerry Stahl uses the term for collaborative knowledge building in which meaning emerges through small-group interaction, particularly in technology-mediated settings.<ref name="stahl">[https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262195393/group-cognition/ Stahl, G. (2006), ''Group Cognition: Computer Support for Building Collaborative Knowledge'', MIT Press.]</ref>

Team-cognition research uses related concepts such as shared mental models, transactive memory, team situation awareness, and interactive team cognition.

== Group as unit of analysis ==

Suppose individuals <math>A_1,\ldots,A_n</math> contribute utterances, actions, and artifacts.

A purely individual analysis asks what each person knows.

A group-cognition analysis also asks:

* how information moves between members;
* which contributions change later reasoning;
* how misunderstandings are repaired;
* how artifacts stabilize shared reference;
* how roles and expertise interact.

The group process can have structure not visible in isolated individual scores.

== Interaction ==

Interaction is central.

One participant proposes an idea.

Another challenges it.

A third reframes the problem.

The first participant then revises the proposal.

The final solution may not have existed fully in any individual before the interaction.

Group cognition studies this sequence rather than assigning the final result to one person retrospectively.

== Shared mental models ==

A '''shared mental model''' is overlapping understanding among team members about relevant aspects of a task, system, roles, or coordination.

Shared models can help members anticipate one another without constant explicit communication.

Examples include shared expectations about:

* goals;
* equipment;
* procedures;
* role responsibilities;
* likely contingencies.

Research distinguishes similarity from accuracy.

A team can share the same incorrect model.

Sharedness therefore does not guarantee good performance.<ref name="smm">[https://doi.org/10.1177/154193129103501917 Converse, S. A., Cannon-Bowers, J. A., and Salas, E. (1991), “Team Member Shared Mental Models: A Theory and Some Methodological Issues.”]</ref>

== Transactive memory ==

A '''transactive memory system''' does not require every member to know the same things.

Instead, members know who possesses which expertise.

A team can therefore distribute memory:

<pre>
member A knows domain X
member B knows domain Y
member C knows who knows X and Y
</pre>

Kyle Lewis developed a widely used measure of transactive memory emphasizing specialization, credibility, and coordination.<ref name="tms">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12940401/ Lewis, K. (2003), “Measuring transactive memory systems in the field,” ''Journal of Applied Psychology'' 88(4), 587–604.]</ref>

Transactive memory is particularly useful when expertise is complementary rather than redundant.

== Shared knowledge versus distributed knowledge ==

Teams need both common and specialized knowledge.

Shared knowledge supports:

* coordination;
* terminology;
* goals;
* expectations.

Specialized knowledge supports:

* division of labor;
* expertise;
* parallel work.

Too little shared knowledge creates communication failures.

Too much redundancy can waste specialization.

Group cognition therefore concerns how the two are balanced.

== Common ground ==

[[Common ground]] is information participants treat as mutually shared for purposes of communication.

Conversation relies on assumptions about:

* what has already been said;
* what terms mean;
* what both participants can see;
* which goals are shared.

Common ground is updated through dialogue.

Misunderstanding occurs when participants incorrectly assume shared context.

== Grounding ==

'''Grounding''' is the process of establishing that a contribution has been understood sufficiently for the current purpose.

Signals include:

* acknowledgment;
* clarification;
* reformulation;
* correction;
* successful next action.

Perfect mutual understanding is unnecessary.

The amount of grounding depends on task risk and communication cost.

== Interactive team cognition ==

Cooke and colleagues propose '''Interactive Team Cognition''' (ITC), in which team cognition is treated as dynamic activity emerging through interaction rather than as a static shared property.<ref name="itc">[https://doi.org/10.1111/cogs.12009 Cooke, N. J., Gorman, J. C., Myers, C. W., and Duran, J. L. (2013), “Interactive Team Cognition,” ''Cognitive Science'' 37(2), 255–285.]</ref>

ITC emphasizes three premises:

* team cognition is an activity;
* it should be measured at the team level;
* it is inseparable from context.

This contrasts with approaches that infer team cognition mainly from questionnaires about individual knowledge.

== Coordination ==

Coordination aligns contributions in time and function.

Teams coordinate through:

* explicit communication;
* role assignment;
* routines;
* shared artifacts;
* observation;
* timing;
* prediction.

Well-practiced teams can coordinate with reduced explicit communication because shared expectations and transactive memory reduce the need to explain every action.

Reduced communication is useful only if the underlying expectations are accurate.

== Communication ==

Communication can improve cognition by sharing unique information.

It can also create problems:

* interruption;
* overload;
* dominance;
* conformity;
* repetition of shared information.

More communication is not always better.

The relevant measure is whether interaction supports task-relevant coordination.

== Hidden-profile problem ==

In hidden-profile experiments, each member receives some unique information while the group shares other information.

Groups often discuss shared information more than unique information.

As a result, they can fail to discover the best answer even though the necessary evidence exists collectively.

The phenomenon illustrates that distributed information does not automatically become group knowledge.

== Collaborative knowledge building ==

In collaborative learning, participants can construct knowledge through:

* proposals;
* questions;
* explanations;
* disagreements;
* revisions;
* shared artifacts.

Stahl's group-cognition approach emphasizes analysis of the interaction sequence itself rather than assuming collaborative learning is simply the sum of individual learning.<ref name="stahl" />

A group's artifact history can show conceptual development over time.

== Artifacts ==

Groups use external cognitive artifacts such as:

* whiteboards;
* diagrams;
* documents;
* issue trackers;
* spreadsheets;
* maps;
* shared code.

Artifacts stabilize information and allow participants to coordinate asynchronously.

They also preserve prior decisions.

A shared document can become part of the group cognitive process without implying that the document is conscious.

== Technology-mediated group cognition ==

Digital collaboration systems shape interaction.

Interface design controls:

* visibility;
* turn structure;
* persistence;
* editability;
* notification;
* search.

A synchronous video meeting and an asynchronous version-controlled repository create different cognitive environments.

Technology is therefore not a neutral channel.

== Distributed cognition ==

[[Distributed cognition]] analyzes cognition across people, artifacts, and environments.

Group cognition often focuses more narrowly on group interaction and collaborative meaning-making.

The concepts overlap strongly when group reasoning depends on external artifacts.

Distributed cognition can extend the system boundary farther into material infrastructure.

== Situated cognition ==

[[Situated cognition]] emphasizes context-sensitive cognition.

Group cognition is necessarily situated because interaction depends on current task, environment, and social structure.

However, situated cognition also covers individual cognition in context and does not require group interaction.

== Intersubjectivity ==

[[Intersubjectivity]] concerns relations among subjective perspectives and the possibility of shared meaning.

Group cognition studies the processes by which such shared understanding can support coordinated reasoning.

The concepts overlap but are not identical.

Intersubjectivity has stronger philosophical and phenomenological traditions.

== Collective intelligence ==

[[Collective intelligence]] is primarily a performance construct.

A group can display high collective intelligence with little interaction, such as averaging independent estimates.

A group can display rich cognition through discussion while reaching a poor answer.

Thus:

<pre>
group cognition = process and organization
collective intelligence = performance capability
</pre>

The distinction is analytical rather than absolute.

== Team situation awareness ==

Teams operating in dynamic environments need understanding of current state.

Team situation awareness can involve:

* overlapping awareness;
* distributed role-specific awareness;
* awareness of teammates' needs.

The team does not need every member to know everything.

It needs the right information available to the right role at the right time.

== Measurement ==

Group cognition can be measured using:

* communication transcripts;
* interaction networks;
* concept maps;
* recall tests;
* shared mental-model measures;
* transactive-memory scales;
* process coding;
* physiological synchrony;
* artifact histories.

A cross-domain review found many viable measurement methods, each tied to different theoretical assumptions.<ref name="measurement">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25141596/ Wildman, J. L., Salas, E., and Scott, C. P. R. (2014), “Measuring cognition in teams: a cross-domain review,” ''Human Factors'' 56(5), 911–941.]</ref>

No single metric captures all forms of group cognition.

== Process tracing ==

A process trace records time-ordered interaction.

For example:

<pre>
proposal
→ challenge
→ evidence
→ revision
→ agreement
→ action
</pre>

Analysis can ask which transitions predict successful coordination.

This is stronger than measuring only final output when the research question concerns cognition.

== Network analysis ==

Communication can be represented as graph.

Nodes are participants.

Edges represent interaction.

Metrics can identify centralization, subgroups, or brokerage.

A central participant is not automatically the most knowledgeable.

Network position and cognitive contribution should be analyzed separately.

== Leadership ==

Leadership can shape information flow.

Centralized leadership can improve coordination under time pressure.

Distributed leadership can exploit specialized expertise.

Recent team-cognition research treats leadership and team cognition as potentially bidirectional: leadership changes cognition, while team cognitive structure affects who leads.<ref name="leadership">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40819470/ systematic review of collective leadership and team cognition, 2025.]</ref>

== Group memory ==

Groups preserve memory through:

* individual members;
* shared documents;
* routines;
* archives;
* roles.

Group memory can outlast individual participation.

Loss of a key expert can still destroy tacit components.

Organizational memory therefore depends on both redundancy and transfer.

== Conflict ==

Cognitive conflict can improve reasoning when disagreement targets ideas and evidence.

Interpersonal conflict can reduce coordination.

Teams benefit when dissent can be expressed without social penalty.

A uniform group can appear harmonious while suppressing important information.

== Groupthink ==

Groupthink refers to dysfunctional consensus-seeking under certain social conditions.

Possible warning signs include:

* self-censorship;
* pressure on dissenters;
* illusion of unanimity;
* failure to examine alternatives.

The concept is historically influential but should not be used as a catch-all explanation for every bad group decision.

Specific mechanisms should be measured.

== Error propagation ==

One member's error can spread through interaction.

If others trust the speaker or repeat the claim, an initially local error can become shared.

Group consensus therefore increases confidence only when information sources and verification are sufficiently independent.

== Human–AI teams ==

Artificial agents can participate in group cognition by:

* retrieving information;
* summarizing;
* suggesting plans;
* monitoring state;
* generating alternatives.

Teams can develop shared expectations about an AI system.

Human–AI shared mental models are useful only when the model's actual capabilities and limitations are represented accurately.

Overtrust creates coordination failure.

== Multi-agent artificial groups ==

A group of AI agents can exchange messages and divide tasks.

Process analysis should trace:

* dependencies;
* duplicated evidence;
* shared models;
* error correction;
* final aggregation.

Ten agents using the same underlying model are not ten independent sources.

Coordinated output does not imply collective consciousness.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Group cognition is a legitimate level of analysis when interaction materially contributes to cognitive performance.

It should not be reified into a mysterious group mind.

A rigorous analysis identifies the actual interaction, representations, roles, artifacts, and transformations through which group-level reasoning emerges.

